---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { LINKS } from '../site/links'
---
<Base title="Privacy: Cranley" description="What this site collects, what the event demo keeps, and what running Cranley yourself means for your data.">
  <article class="page">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <p class="intro">{LINKS.demo
        ? 'The pages of this site are static: no accounts, no analytics, no advertising and no cookies. The live demo is the one part that takes anything from you: what you type into it. It is described below.'
        : 'This site is a set of static pages about a hackathon project. It has no forms, no accounts, no analytics, no advertising and no cookies.'}</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="small muted">Last updated November 14, 2026.</p>

        <h2>What this site collects</h2>
        <p>Nothing that you type. The site asks for no name, email address or other detail.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Hosting.</strong> Vercel serves these pages. To deliver a page, any host receives your IP address, your browser's type and the address of the page, and Vercel keeps logs of this under its own privacy policy. We add no analytics and do not use those logs to identify anyone.</li>
          <li><strong>One setting in your browser.</strong> If you change the colour scheme in the docs, your browser remembers the choice in its local storage under the name <code>starlight-theme</code>. It never leaves your device.</li>
          <li><strong>Search.</strong> The search in the docs runs inside your browser on an index downloaded from this site. What you search for is not sent to us.</li>
          <li><strong>Fonts and scripts.</strong> They are served from this site. The pages load nothing from another company, with one exception: the videos on the track pages are YouTube's player, loaded from youtube-nocookie.com, YouTube's privacy-enhanced address. Opening a track page that has a video sends your IP address and browser type to YouTube, and Google's privacy policy covers what it does with them.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Links to other sites</h2>
        <p>The links to GitHub, Devpost and the sources we credit lead to sites with their own privacy policies.</p>

        {LINKS.demo && (
          <>
            <h2>The event demo</h2>
            <p>For HackPrinceton there is a <a href={LINKS.demo}>live demo</a> that is open to anyone until the event ends, with no code and no sign-in. It runs on our own server; this site passes your requests on to it. Opening it in a browser sets no cookie. Opening the phone link that the app shows as a QR code sets one, <code>cranley_demo</code>, which lasts three days and holds a random visitor number used only to count requests. The demo asks for no name and no email address. What you type there is sent to our server and to the language model it uses. The demo keeps two things: a log of each request's IP address and path, which it needs to limit how often it can be asked, and the workspaces that visitors create. Both are deleted when the demo is switched off after the event. The building data in it is used under the event's terms and is deleted after the event.</p>
          </>
        )}

        <h2>If you run Cranley yourself</h2>
        <p>{LINKS.demo ? 'Apart from the event demo, there is no hosted Cranley.' : 'There is no hosted Cranley.'} The software runs on a machine you control, and so does its data.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>What you type as a need is sent to your own server and to the language model you configured for it.</li>
          <li>A scan from the iPhone app, and anything a paired phone measures (its location, photos, sound level, tilt and typed values), is stored in your server's data folder or in the databases you connected.</li>
          <li>Scans of real buildings from Matterport, Vexcel or CoStar come under your own agreement with those companies.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Questions</h2>
        <p>Open an issue on <a href={`${LINKS.github}/issues`}>GitHub</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
